{
 "question": "Where does transient pre-noise processing put a correction, and with which windows does it fade it in and out?",
 "method": "Clause E.2.7 counts the transient from \"the first decoded PCM sample contained in the audio frame\" and leaves the two windows open. A correction is the decode itself, copied and faded, so both can be read off a reference decode: `tools/tpnp_fit.py` decodes a stream without the tool, applies the correction of the clause under a hypothesis (the frame begins at sample 0, 256 or 512 of the samples oadec hands out for it; the windows are straight lines or raised cosines) and measures how far the result is from the reference over the corrected samples. The references are two Dolby decoders: the `dlbac3dec` element of Dolby Reference Player 3.2 (`drc-suppress=true`) and the `ddp_decode` filter of the Dolby Encoding Engine 5.2.1 (`drc` none). The streams: two of the encoder of evidence 28, whose signals were made for it (in one the place of each level step is known from the source file); one of the Dolby Encoding Engine; the heads of two film tracks. `tools/tpnp_window.py`, the measurement the notes quoted before, counts over the samples the tool changes. `tools/old_new.py --decode-all` compared main with the change over the corpus. The streams are media and are not in the repository.",
